  "query_preview": "..A ridge of high pressure will try to build into \nthe area today but it remains weak as it tries to build into the \narea. As a result, it will be short lived across the region as a \ntrough/low pressure starts to develop over the eastern Pacific. A \nfew lingering showers or thunderstorms may develop through this \nafternoon across the higher terrain but expect most areas to remain \ndry with warm temperatures continuing. \n\nThe weather shifts over the weekend as we see tropical moisture and \na couple of troughs/lows move into the region. The low over the \neastern Pacific is cut off and starts to interact with what will \neventually become the remnants of TS Rachel. This system will be \nslow to move on Saturday, but as a trough digs down from the Pacific \nNorthwest, it will begins it push to the northeast and towards the \nDesert Southwest. The latest model runs take Rachel and start \nbringing the moisture inland on Saturday in the deepening south-\nsouthwest flow, with the bulk of moisture arriving late Saturday and \nSunday. This moisture will hang around through the weekend and into \nnext week, with periods of rain, some of which may be heavy, \nespecially over western Arizona. The tropical remnants look to push \nto the northeast and out of the area by late Sunday, but the \ndeepening PacNW trough will dive southward along the California \ncoast, and move through the area early in the week, possibly not \nexiting fully until Wednesday. Certainly a pretty unsettled period \nfor our region. \n\nQPF ranges remain fairly widespread but the potential for 1-3\" with \nlocally higher amounts are possible. Current projections based on \nthe path for Rachel put the highest amounts across Yavapai and \nwestern Coconino counties with higher amounts to the west in Mohave \nCounty. There remains some uncertainty as to whether we will see \nprolonged periods of light to moderate rain or more robust heavy \nrainfall along with thunderstorms through the event, the latter of \nwhich could lead to flash flood concerns. As Rachel pushes to the \nnortheast, we should be able to get a better picture of where the \nbest moisture advection will take place. With that in mind we will \nhold off on any flood watches at this time.\n\nAnother thing to keep an eye on will be the increasing south to \nsouthwest winds as the gradient really tightens up with Rachel \npushing towards the SoCal coast and the deepening PacNW trough. \nWinds look to be strongest in the Sunday through Tuesday time frame. \nWinds will start to increase on Saturday but become stronger on \nSunday, where we will see south to southwest winds 15-25 mph with \ngusts 35-45 mph. Winds look to remain strong on Monday but then \nstart to taper off on Tuesday. Wind Advisories may be needed so stay \ntuned for updates. Keep any eye on the forecast as we get more \nrefined over the next day or two. \n\nBeyond midweek, a ridge looks to build into the region, bringing \nback dry conditions, lighter winds, and warmer temperatures.",
